    "q": "What counts as a family heirloom?",
    "a": "<p>Any item a family keeps and hands down because of who it belonged to: furniture, jewelry, silver, quilts, letters, recipes, tools, photographs. An heirloom does not need a market value. Many of the most important ones have none.</p>"
  },
  {
    "q": "What is the best way to record an heirloom&rsquo;s story?",
    "a": "<p>Ask the person who knows it, soon, and write down their answer in their own words, with the date and who told it. Add a photo of the item and, if possible, an old photo of it in use. Keep the story with the item&rsquo;s record rather than in a separate notebook.</p>"
  },
  {
    "q": "How do families decide who gets which heirloom?",
    "a": "<p>The smoothest way is for the owner to decide while they can, talk about it openly, and write it down. Ask each person what matters to them before assuming; the item one person loves most is often not the one with the highest price. Where wishes need legal force, an estate attorney can advise on a will or, in many states, a separate written list of personal property.</p>"
  },
  {
    "q": "What is a personal property memorandum?",
    "a": "<p>In many US states, a will can refer to a separate signed list that says who receives particular items of tangible personal property, such as jewelry or furniture. The list can usually be updated without rewriting the will. Rules differ by state, so ask an estate attorney whether it applies to you.</p>"
  },
  {
    "q": "Should I insure family heirlooms?",
    "a": "<p>If an heirloom would be expensive to replace, check your policy&rsquo;s limits for categories such as jewelry, silverware and art. Items above them can often be covered separately, and insurers usually ask for a written appraisal from an independent appraiser. An heirloom with no market value cannot be replaced by insurance at all, which is one more reason to document its story.</p>"
  },
